.TH NKG 1 "September 2026" "nkg 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nkg \- ranked trigram code search
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nkg index
.I path
.RB [ \-\-index
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B nkg serve
.B \-\-index
.I FILE
.B \-\-port
.I PORT
.br
.B nkg
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-w ]
.RB [ \-F ]
.RB [ \-c ]
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR NUM ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR PAT ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-format
.IR json | text ]
.RB [ \-\-color ]
.RB [ \-.
.RB | \-\-hidden ]
.RB [ \-\-no-ignore ]
.RB [ \-L ]
.RB [ \-g
.IR GLOB ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-max-filesize
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-A
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-B
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-C
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-group-separator
.IR SEP ]
.RB [ \-\-top
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-use-index
.IR FILE " | " \-\-port
.IR PORT ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I pattern
.RI [ path ]
.br
.B nkg
.RB [ options ]
.B \-\-
.I pattern
.B \-
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nkg
searches code with a trigram index that finds candidate files, then a
best-first verify step ranks them. On covered query classes (plain
literals) every result set equals ripgrep.
.PP
The default search prints one JSON object per match on stdout:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{"path":"a.txt","line":1,"text":"hello world","score":-4.000001}
.fi
.RE
.PP
A diagnostics line always goes to stderr:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nkg: 2 matches in 2 files, 2 ms (index load 0 ms)
.fi
.RE
.PP
The search root defaults to the current directory when reading from a
terminal; an explicit
.B \-
operand, or piped stdin with no
.I path
operand, searches stdin as one unit labeled
.BR "(standard input)" .
.PP
With any
.B \-e
or
.B \-f
option, patterns come from the options and all positionals are paths
(zero or one supported): the leading
.I pattern
operand form above does not apply.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore-case
Case-insensitive match (full simple-fold insensitivity).
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet ", " \-\-silent
Suppress stdout and stop at the first match. Stderr keeps the
diagnostics line. Exits 0 on the first match, 1 on none.
.B \-\-silent
is an alias for
.BR \-\-quiet .
.PP
Short flags combine within their group:
.B \-iq
(and any cluster using only
.B i
and
.B q
letters) behaves as
.B \-i
plus
.B \-q ;
.B \-cl
(and any cluster using only
.B c
and
.B l
letters) behaves as
.B \-c
plus
.B \-l .
Mixed clusters (for example
.B \-ci
) stay positional.
.TP
.B \-
Read standard input instead of
.I path .
.TP
.BR \-e " PAT" ", " \-\-regexp " PAT" ", " \-\-regexp=PAT
Search for
.IR PAT .
Repeatable: each occurrence adds an alternation branch (logical OR).
A
.B (?i)
flag group applies to its own pattern only, not to the whole query.
With any
.B \-e
or
.B \-f ,
positionals are paths (zero or one supported) instead of
.IR pattern " " [ path ].
.TP
.BR \-f " FILE" ", " \-\-file " FILE" ", " \-\-file=FILE
Read patterns from
.IR FILE ,
one per line; repeatable, unioned with
.B \-e
patterns. An empty line matches every line. A
.B \-
operand reads patterns from stdin. A missing file exits 2.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-fixed-strings
Treat all patterns (positional,
.B \-e ,
.B \-f )
as literal strings, never regex.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-invert-match
Print non-matching lines instead of matches. Forces a full scan:
the trigram index is not consulted.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-word-regexp
Match whole words only: patterns are wrapped in Unicode word
boundaries.
.TP
.BR \-m " NUM" ", " \-\-max-count " NUM" ", " \-\-max-count=NUM
Stop collecting after
.I NUM
matches per file; 0 matches nothing.
Also spelled
.BR \-m NUM " (attached)."
Applies before
.B \-\-top ;
.B \-c
prints the capped counts. An invalid number exits 2.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-count
Print
.B path:count
per matching file, path-sorted.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-files-with-matches
Print matching paths only, path-sorted.
.B \-l
wins over
.BR \-c .
.TP
.BI "\-\-format " "json|text"
Output rendering (default
.BR json ).
.B text
emits
.B path:line:text
in rank order: the same hits in the same order as
.BR json ,
without the score field. Ignored under
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-q .
.TP
.BI "\-\-color" "[=WHEN]"
Highlight match spans in
.B \-\-format text
(default
.BR auto :
colorize only when stdout is a terminal).
.B always
and
.B never
force or suppress;
.B ansi
aliases
.BR always .
A bare
.B \-\-color
forces color on; a space-separated word
.RB ( \-\-color " always" )
is consumed as the value when it is one of
.BR auto ", " always ", " never ", " ansi .
JSON output is never colorized, and
.B GREP_COLORS
is not honored. An unknown
.I WHEN
exits 2.
.TP
.BR "\-." ", " \-\-hidden
Search hidden files and directories (skipped by default).
.TP
.B \-\-no-ignore
Skip
.BR .gitignore ", " .ignore ", " .rgignore ,
global excludes, and parent ignore files: search everything the
traversal reaches (subject to
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-d ,
and
.BR \-\-max-filesize ).
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-follow
Follow symbolic links during traversal (unfollowed by default).
.TP
.BR \-g " GLOB" ", " \-\-glob " GLOB" ", " \-\-glob=GLOB
Include or exclude paths with a gitignore-style glob. Repeatable;
a leading
.B !
excludes (for example
.BR "\-g '!*.min.js'" ).
Used alone, negations keep everything else; bare globs whitelist.
Also spelled
.BR \-gGLOB " (attached)."
Ignore rules still apply above
.B \-g
whitelists: a gitignored file matching
.B \-g
stays skipped.
.TP
.BR \-d " N" ", " \-\-max-depth " N" ", " \-\-max-depth=N
Limit directory traversal depth. Also spelled
.BR \-dN " (attached)."
An invalid number exits 2.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max-filesize " N
Skip files larger than
.I N
bytes; accepts
.BR K ", " M ", " G
suffixes (powers of 1024, either case: 1K keeps a 1010-byte file).
Also spelled
.BR \-\-max-filesize= N .
Garbage exits 2.
.TP
.BR \-A " N" ", " \-\-after-context " N" ", " \-\-after-context=N
Print
.I N
lines after each match. Also spelled
.BR \-AN " (attached: " \-A2 ")."
An invalid number exits 2.
.TP
.BR \-B " N" ", " \-\-before-context " N" ", " \-\-before-context=N
Print
.I N
lines before each match. Also spelled
.BR \-BN " (attached)."
An invalid number exits 2.
.TP
.BR \-C " N" ", " \-\-context " N" ", " \-\-context=N
Print
.I N
lines around each match. Also spelled
.BR \-CN " (attached)."
An explicit
.B \-A
or
.B \-B
beats
.B \-C
on its side. An invalid number exits 2.
.TP
.BR "\-\-group-separator " SEP ", " \-\-group-separator=SEP
Separator printed between disjoint context groups in one file
(default
.BR \-\- ).
An empty value prints no separator lines.
.TP
.BI \-\-top " N"
Print only the top
.I N
ranked matches.
.TP
.BI \-\-use-index " FILE"
Load this index file for the query, consulting the daemon registered
for it first and falling back to a local index load when the server
is unreachable.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
Query the daemon on
.I PORT
instead of searching locally.
.TP
.B \-\-
End the flag scan, so a pattern beginning with
.B \-
is searched literally.
.TP
.BI \-\-index " FILE"
For
.B index
and
.BR serve ,
use
.I FILE
as the index file (default:
.BR .nkg.json ).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help to stdout and exit 0.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version to stdout and exit 0.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "index " path " [" \-\-index " " FILE ]
Walk
.I path
and write the trigram index to
.IR FILE .
Binary files (NUL in the first 8192 bytes) are skipped.
.TP
.BI "serve \-\-index " FILE " \-\-port " PORT
Serve the index on 127.0.0.1:
.IR PORT .
Registers the port in
.IB FILE .serve.json
for
.B \-\-use-index
clients.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one match (or
.BR \-\-help / \-\-version
output). A closed stdout pipe (for example
.BR "| head" )
also exits 0 quietly.
.TP
.B 1
No matches.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, bad regex, invalid
.B \-m
.RB / \-d /
.B \-A
.RB / \-B /
.B \-C
number, bad
.B \-\-format
.RB / \-\-color /
.B \-\-max-filesize
value, missing
.B \-f
file, unreadable or stale index, unresolvable root,
bind/connect failure, or other I/O error. Usage errors print the
usage text to stderr; bad patterns and values report as
.BR "nkg: " msg .
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.nf
nkg index . \-\-index nkg.idx.json
nkg \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-i \-\- 'hello.*world' .
nkg \-\-top 20 \-\- 'TODO|FIXME' .
nkg \-c \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-l \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-q \-\- 'pattern' src && echo found
printf 'hello piped\\n' | nkg \-\- 'hello'
nkg \-e 'TODO' \-e 'FIXME' src
nkg \-w \-\- 'word' src
nkg \-\-format text \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-\-format text \-\-color=always \-\- 'pattern' src | cat
nkg \-A 2 \-B 1 \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-C 1 \-\-group-separator '' \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-g '*.rs' \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-g '!*.min.js' \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-\-hidden \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-\-no-ignore \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-L \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-d 2 \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-\-max-filesize 1M \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-F \-\- 'literal (parens)' src
nkg \-v \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-m 5 \-\- 'pattern' src
nkg \-\- 'pattern' \- < input.txt
nkg serve \-\-index nkg.idx.json \-\-port 17632 &
nkg \-\-port 17632 \-\-top 20 \-\- 'pattern'
nkg \-\-use-index nkg.idx.json \-\- 'pattern' .
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rg (1),
.BR grep (1)
